You give a coding agent such as Claude Code or Codex a long task in your laptop's terminal window, then accidentally close it. The agent may exit before it finishes.

The fix is tmux, a terminal multiplexer. Start a named tmux **session**, a workspace you can leave and return to, with `tmux new -s agent`. Launch the agent inside it. If you close the window, open another terminal on the same machine and run `tmux attach -t agent`. You return to the running session, its current screen and recent output kept as **scrollback**.

A background process, the **tmux server**, keeps the agent's terminal open while you are disconnected. It runs on the same machine as the agent. That machine must stay running: tmux does not preserve processes through a reboot ([tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1)).

{% takeaways %}
- tmux keeps your programs connected to terminals that stay open after you close the visible window.
- It remembers what each part of the screen should show, so it can redraw your workspace when you return.
- A quiet coding agent may be working or waiting for you. Output alone cannot reliably tell you which.
{% /takeaways %}

## The window, the shell and the program

Your **terminal window** is the visible interface of a terminal app, such as Ghostty or iTerm2. The app displays text and sends your keystrokes. Your **shell**, such as bash or zsh, is a separate program that reads commands and starts other programs. At a bash prompt, bash reads what you type. After you start vim, vim receives your typing instead.

Unix connects these programs to the terminal app through a **pseudo-terminal**, or **PTY**. Think of it as a two-way cable made in software. A PTY also gives programs a terminal interface: they can query its size and control how input is buffered, echoed or turned into signals ([`pty(7)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pty.7.html)).

A PTY has two ends:

- The **master** end connects to the terminal window. The window writes keystrokes here and reads output from it.
- The **slave** end connects to the programs running inside the terminal. On Linux, it has a path such as `/dev/pts/3`.

The **foreground program** is the one currently using the terminal for input. In the usual shell-and-editor example, that is bash at the prompt or vim while you edit. The shell launches vim and waits; vim reads and writes the same terminal itself. A foreground job can also contain several processes, such as a pipeline ([Bash job control](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Job-Control-Basics.html)).

Between the PTY ends sits the **line discipline**, a set of terminal rules in the **kernel**, the core of the operating system. Its settings control what happens to the bytes passing through ([Linux TTY documentation](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/tty/index.html)).

![Keys travel down through one PTY's master, kernel line discipline and slave to the foreground program, while output travels up; a dashed side arrow shows the shell launching vim and waiting.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-stack-without-tmux.png)

## How tmux divides a workspace

A **terminal multiplexer** lets you use several terminals through one visible terminal window. tmux organises them like this:

| Object | What it holds |
|---|---|
| Session | A named workspace containing one or more windows |
| Window | One or more panes arranged together, like a tab containing splits |
| Pane | A rectangular terminal area with its own PTY and running program |

A pane usually starts with a shell. That shell can launch an editor, an agent or a pipeline, just as it would outside tmux. Splitting a window creates another pane and another PTY. Several people or terminal windows can attach to the same session ([tmux Getting Started](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Getting-Started)).

Try running `tty`, which prints your terminal's device path, before starting tmux, then again inside it. The paths differ. With the default bindings, press `Ctrl-b`, release both keys, then press `%` to split the window. Run `tty` in the new pane: it has another path. Each pane gives its programs a separate terminal.

### The client attaches; the server keeps running

Running `tmux` starts a **client**, the program that attaches your terminal to a session. If necessary, it also starts the server. The **server** runs in the background and owns the sessions and their pane PTYs. Both processes run on the same machine.

They communicate through a **Unix domain socket**, a local communication endpoint that programs use to exchange messages. The socket has a filesystem path, but messages sent through it are not saved like an ordinary file. Here, it carries control messages such as attach requests and resize notifications.

During attachment, the client passes the server access to your terminal's connection. The server then reads keys and writes screen updates through that connection. The client remains running to handle events such as terminal resizing. It does not relay each keystroke through the socket.

The original PTY still exists between your terminal window and tmux. Call this the **outer PTY**. The window holds its master end; the server uses its slave end. On the other side, the server holds a separate PTY master for each pane.

![The terminal window connects through an outer PTY to the tmux server, which holds three in-memory screen grids and three pane PTYs; a separate control socket connects the client to the server.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-stack-with-tmux.png)

The side socket carries control messages. At attach time, the client passes terminal access to the server; keys and screen updates then use the direct PTY connection. Terminal settings are omitted from this diagram.

## From program output to a screen

Terminal programs send text mixed with **escape sequences**: instructions that start with the Escape control character. They can move the cursor or change the colour of the text that follows. The terminal interprets these instructions instead of printing them as words.

These examples use spaces for readability. `ESC` means the Escape character, not the three letters `E`, `S`, `C`; the spaces shown are not part of the sequences ([xterm control sequences](https://mirrors.mit.edu/NetBSD/NetBSD-release-11/xsrc/external/mit/xterm/dist/ctlseqs.txt)).

| Sequence | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `ESC [ 2 ; 5 H` | Move the cursor to row 2, column 5 |
| `ESC [ 31 m` | Use the terminal's red text colour |
| `ESC [ ? 1049 h` | Switch to an alternate screen buffer, keeping the previous screen contents to restore when the program leaves it |

The alternate screen buffer is why quitting vim usually brings your earlier shell screen back.

Try this at a shell prompt, inside or outside tmux:

```sh
printf '\033[31mred\033[0m\n'
```

`\033` supplies the Escape character. The first sequence selects red text; the second resets the styling. The terminal displays the word `red` in red and consumes the formatting instructions.

For each pane, tmux interprets this output and maintains a **screen grid** in memory. The grid stores text and its appearance at screen positions; some characters occupy more than one cell. tmux also tracks the cursor position. It uses those grids to draw the visible panes and status bar into your terminal window.

This work is **terminal emulation**: interpreting a program's output as instructions for a terminal screen. tmux contains a terminal emulator for its panes; your window's terminal emulator, such as Ghostty or iTerm2, displays the combined result. A program in one pane can clear its own screen without erasing the neighbouring panes.

### Updating one cell

Suppose a pane's screen is blank. Its program sends the instruction to move to row 2, column 5, followed by the letter `X`. tmux updates that cell in the pane's grid.

Row 2, column 5 is relative to the pane. tmux translates that position into the pane's location in your terminal window, then can move the terminal cursor there and print `X` without redrawing the other cells. For example, if six columns and a one-column divider sit to its left, the pane's column 5 becomes terminal column 12.

![tmux places X at pane row 2, column 5. In the terminal, the left pane occupies columns 1 through 6 and the divider occupies column 7, so the right pane spans columns 8 through 13 and X appears at row 2, column 12. Only the changed cell is redrawn.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/pane-screen-grid-update.png)

The grid records the screen's current contents. Scrollback keeps a limited amount of older output. Neither is a recording of every intermediate screen the program drew. tmux can update part of the display as output arrives, or redraw the visible layout when you attach again.

## How input reaches the right program

The line discipline's settings determine what the program receives. With **echo** enabled, the kernel sends typed characters back towards the window so you can see them. In **canonical mode**, it collects a line before delivering it: Backspace edits that waiting line, and Enter submits it. A simple program such as `cat` can use this without implementing its own input editor.

A **signal** is an operating-system notification to a process. With terminal signal generation enabled, Ctrl-C sends **SIGINT**, an interruption request, to the foreground job. A program may handle or ignore it, or change the terminal settings so Ctrl-C arrives as input instead ([`termios(3)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/termios.3.html)).

Full-screen editors use raw or similar input settings to receive keys without waiting for Enter. Interactive bash and zsh also read keys individually and draw their own command-line edits, with kernel echo disabled. Line buffering, echo and signal generation have separate controls.

tmux disables all three on the outer PTY, so it receives input directly. Each pane has its own terminal settings. When tmux forwards Ctrl-C into a pane, that pane's settings determine whether the kernel turns it into SIGINT or delivers it as input. This also prevents the outer PTY from echoing characters twice or holding them until Enter.

### Follow a single keypress

At an interactive bash prompt inside tmux, press `x`:

1. Your terminal window writes `x` to the outer PTY's master end.
2. The tmux server reads it through the outer PTY's slave end.
3. The server checks its key bindings. By default, `Ctrl-b` is the **prefix**, a key combination that makes the following key a tmux command. A plain `x` passes through.
4. The server writes `x` to the active pane's PTY master.
5. Bash reads it from the slave end. Bash's line editor echoes `x` itself, sending output back through that pane's PTY.
6. The server interprets the output, updates the pane's grid and sends the corresponding screen update through the outer PTY.

![Typing x at a bash prompt follows six steps through the outer PTY, tmux's binding check, the active pane's PTY, bash's echo and the screen-grid update.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-keypress-roundtrip.png)

Scripts can use the same machinery. `tmux send-keys` sends input to a pane, while `tmux capture-pane -p` prints its current screen as text. A script could send a command and inspect the output with these operations; it would still need a way to tell when the command had finished ([OpenBSD tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1)).

## Close the window. The pane stays open.

Without tmux, closing the window closes its end of the PTY. The operating system's terminal hang-up handling can send **SIGHUP**, the hang-up signal, to processes associated with that terminal. Shells can pass it on to their jobs. Whether a particular program exits depends on its signal handling and shell settings ([POSIX `close()`](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/close.html), [Bash signals](https://www.gnu.org/s/bash/manual/html_node/Signals.html)).

With tmux, the outer terminal connection can close while the server keeps the pane PTYs open. The server runs independently of the terminal that started it. Losing an attached client therefore leaves the agent connected to its pane's terminal.

The same separation helps over **SSH**, which lets you log into another machine. In this case you start tmux and the agent on the remote machine. The remote SSH service holds the master end of your login PTY. Once it detects a lost connection and closes that PTY, the login shell and attached tmux client can exit. The tmux server still holds the separate pane PTYs on that machine.

![The old SSH login and tmux client end together, while the server, pane PTYs and programs survive on the remote machine; a new SSH connection and tmux attach -t agent reconnect through a new client.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-ssh-drop.png)

SSH back into that machine and run `tmux attach -t agent` to return to the session named `agent`. A fresh client connects to the existing server, which redraws the current screen. You can also detach on purpose: with the default keys, press `Ctrl-b`, release both keys, then press `d`.

The machine and server must keep running. Rebooting the machine or killing the tmux server ends this protection. If your laptop sleeps, local programs pause until it wakes; work running on a separate remote machine can continue.

[GNU screen](https://www.gnu.org/software/screen/manual/screen.html) and Zellij also support detaching from running terminal work. Zellij additionally offers [session resurrection](https://zellij.dev/documentation/session-resurrection.html): it can recreate a saved layout and offer to rerun commands after the session ends. Those commands start new processes; they do not recover the old processes' memory.

## Resizing follows the same connections

A terminal's size is a number of rows and columns. Dragging the window bigger sets off another exchange:

1. The terminal window updates the outer PTY's size. The kernel sends **SIGWINCH**, the window-size-change signal, to the foreground job, which includes the tmux client.
2. The client notifies the server over the socket. The server reads the new size and recalculates the pane layout.
3. The server asks the kernel to update each affected pane's terminal size. The kernel notifies that pane's foreground job, allowing vim or another full-screen program to redraw.

Two clients can attach with different terminal sizes. tmux's `window-size` option controls which size a shared window uses; the default, `latest`, follows the most recently used client ([tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1)).

## Colours, copying and scrolling

Programs inside tmux see the terminal tmux provides, which may support different features from your terminal app.

The `TERM` environment variable identifies the terminal type. Programs use it to look up supported features in a database called **terminfo**. Outside tmux, it describes your terminal app. Inside, tmux sets a type it supports, such as `tmux-256color` or `screen-256color`. The matching terminfo entry must be installed where the program runs ([tmux manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tmux.1.html)).

- **Colours look dull.** tmux may not know that your outer terminal supports 24-bit colour. If that terminal supports it, tmux's `terminal-features` option can declare the `RGB` capability. Changing `TERM` to an unrelated terminal type can cause other problems ([tmux FAQ](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/FAQ)).
- **Copied text stays inside tmux.** tmux's copy buffer and your desktop clipboard are separate. **OSC 52** is an escape sequence that lets tmux ask your terminal to update the desktop clipboard, even over SSH, if the terminal permits it. The [Clipboard guide](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Clipboard) explains the settings on both sides.
- **The mouse wheel misses the history you want.** tmux stores history per pane; your outer terminal sees the combined display. Enter tmux's **copy mode**, which lets you browse and select pane history, with `Ctrl-b` then `[`. Press Escape to leave with emacs-style copy-mode bindings, or `q` with vi-style bindings ([copy-mode keys](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1#WINDOWS_AND_PANES)). The `history-limit` option controls retained lines; older output beyond that limit is unavailable ([Getting Started](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Getting-Started)).
- **Mouse selection behaves differently.** With `mouse on`, tmux handles mouse events. Your terminal may provide a modifier key to bypass that handling, such as Shift in many Linux terminals or Option in iTerm2 ([tmux FAQ](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/FAQ)).
- **Escape feels slow in vim.** An Escape key can also begin a longer key sequence. tmux waits to distinguish them; `escape-time` controls that delay ([tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1)).

## Is the agent working or waiting for you?

An agent stops printing. It might be working on a response or waiting for permission to run a command.

tmux can monitor output activity and silence, and report the current command name when available. With a tmux version that supports **OSC 133** command tracking and a shell configured to emit those escape sequences, it can also track command boundaries and exit status.

For a long-running agent, those shell markers describe the agent process's run. They do not tell you whether it is thinking, waiting for approval or finished with one task while still open ([tmux status fields](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tmux.1.html)).

The `pane_dead` flag identifies a pane whose initial process has exited and which tmux has kept open with `remain-on-exit`. Normally, tmux removes the pane when that process exits. If the pane starts bash and you launch an agent from its prompt, the agent can exit while bash and the pane remain alive. The flag therefore cannot tell you whether that agent has finished ([tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1#remain-on-exit)).

An **agent-aware tool** adds information about the work inside a pane. It might recognise a permission question on the screen and mark the agent as blocked. Or the agent might explicitly report that it needs approval. Recognising screen text can fail when the interface changes; an explicit report avoids that guess, though it still depends on the agent reporting correctly.

The tool can show idle, working, blocked or done alongside the source of that status: reported by the agent, or inferred from observations. Inferred states should be marked as guesses.

![An agent-aware tool combines observations and reports: it infers from terminal observations and uses reported status from agents. It shows idle, working, blocked or done with the source labelled reported or inferred, and marks inferred states as guesses.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-what-tmux-sees.png)

For example, Herdr uses process detection and rules that read an agent's screen, with explicit reports taking precedence when available ([Herdr docs](https://herdr.dev/tui/docs/preview/agents/)). Zellij includes both terminal multiplexing and attention tracking: its inbox combines reported status, shell markers and output-based inference to flag panes that may need you ([Zellij docs](https://zellij.dev/documentation/inbox.html)).

You can close a window without abandoning a running agent because tmux keeps its terminal open and remembers its screen. To tell whether it needs approval, look for a permission prompt or an explicit status report.

tmux keeps the agent's process running. What the agent knows from earlier sessions comes from its own memory system, which [How AI Agents Remember](https://blog.carnav.in/how-ai-agents-remember/) explains.

The next post covers **agentic development environments**: agent-aware tools that help you manage the work running in those terminals.

## Sources and further reading

- **Terminal behaviour:** [`pty(7)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pty.7.html), [`pts(4)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html) and the [POSIX terminal interface](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap11.html) describe PTY endpoints, foreground jobs and terminal settings.
- **tmux's implementation:** [client.c](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/client.c) passes terminal access to the server; [tty.c](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/tty.c) disables outer-terminal line buffering, echo and terminal-generated signals; [window.c](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/window.c) manages panes and their virtual screens. [proc.c](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/proc.c) contains the background-process setup.
- **Resizing:** [`TIOCSWINSZ`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/TIOCSWINSZ.2const.html) is the system interface used to set a terminal's size and notify its foreground job.
- **Using tmux:** the [Getting Started guide](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Getting-Started) covers everyday commands; the [manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tmux.1.html) documents options, sockets and status fields.

*The hero image and diagrams were made with AI image generation.*
